If you’re learning Linux or DevOps, one of the first things you should understand is how Linux organizes its filesystem.



 Unlike Windows, everything in Linux starts from / (root) — and each directory has a specific purpose.



Here’s a beginner-friendly breakdown 👇



🔹 /etc

 Stores all system and application configuration files.

 Think of it as the control center for services.



🔹 /usr

 Contains installed software, libraries, and binaries.

 Most applications you install live here.



🔹 /var

 Holds variable data like logs, cache files, and spools.

 📌 /var/log is especially important for troubleshooting.



🔹 /bin

 Includes essential non-admin commands (like ls, cp, cat).

 Available to all users.



🔹 /sbin

 Contains system administration (root) commands.

 Used mainly for system maintenance.



🔹 /proc

 A virtual filesystem that provides real-time system and hardware information.

 Great for monitoring processes and kernel data.



🔹 /home

 Stores user profiles and personal data.

 Each user gets their own directory here.



🔹 /boot

 Contains the Linux kernel and bootloader files.

 Critical for system startup.



💡 Why this matters:

 Understanding the Linux directory structure helps you:

1) Debug issues faster

2) Manage servers confidently

3) Perform better in DevOps & Linux interviews
